# Web Fundamentals & Architecture

## How Web Request Works
- Browser sends request to website
- Needs server IP address → uses DNS

## Process Flow
- DNS resolves domain → gives IP
- Browser connects to web server
- Uses HTTP protocol for communication

## Key Components
- DNS → finds server 
- HTTP → communication
- Server → sends data  
- Browser → displays content  

---

# Load Balancer

## Purpose
- Handles high traffic websites
- Receives request → forwards to one of multiple servers

## Algorithms
- Round Robin → sends requests one by one
- Weighted → sends more traffic to powerful servers

## Features
- Performs health checks
- Stops sending traffic to non-working servers

---

# CDN (Content Delivery Network)

## Function
- Stores static files
- CSS, JS, images, videos

## Benefits
- Uses nearest server to user
- Reduces latency
- Reduces load on main server

---

# Databases

## Purpose
- Store and retrieve data
- Web server communicates with database

## Examples
- MySQL
- MSSQL
- MongoDB

## Range
- Simple files → complex systems

---

# WAF (Web Application Firewall)

## Function
- Sits between user request and web server

## Protection
- Attacks
- DDoS

## Features
- Detects malicious requests
- Checks if request is from real user or bot
- Rate limiting (limits requests per IP)
- Blocks suspicious traffic

---

# Web Server

## Definition
- Software that listens for incoming requests
- Uses HTTP to send web content

## Examples
- Apache
- Nginx
- IIS
- Node.js

## File Locations
- Linux → /var/www/html
- Windows → C:\inetpub\wwwroot

---

# Virtual Hosts

## Concept
- One server can host multiple websites

## Working
- Uses hostname from HTTP headers
- If match found → correct website shown
- If no match → default website shown

## Features
- Each site can have different root directory
- No strict limit on number of websites

---

# Static vs Dynamic Content

## Static Content
- Does not change
- Examples:
  - HTML
  - CSS
  - JS
  - Images
- Same content delivered to all users

## Dynamic Content
- Changes based on user input/request
- Examples:
  - Blog updates
  - Search results

---

# Frontend vs Backend

## Frontend
- What user sees in browser

## Backend
- Processing happens behind the scenes
- Not visible to users

---

# Backend Languages

## Purpose
- Logic
- Database interaction
- Data processing

## Examples
- PHP
- Python
- Ruby
- Node.js
- Perl

